    92	anything unfinished. Chief reads the worklog first when reviewing.
    93	
    94	**Hard rules:** branches only — `gpt/<short-task-name>` off `origin/main`; never commit
    95	to `main`, never merge to `main`, never push `main`. `--no-verify` is forbidden. No new
    96	database migrations (write `PROPOSED_MIGRATION.sql` in your branch and log it). Never
    97	touch `~/.chief/env`, `harness/db/chief.db` (the live record), or anything under `backend/`
    98	/ `frontend/` (v1, frozen). Do exactly what the task says; if a rule here seems wrong,
    99	log it, don't "fix" it. Everything that reaches Neill is plain English (see below).
   100	
   101	**Verification bar** (before DONE): `.venv/bin/python -m pytest harness/tests/ -q` fully
   102	green; working tree clean; commits with clear messages.
   103	
   104	**Gauntlet:** `./scripts/gpt-gauntlet.sh` from your branch — four GPT reviewer seats
   105	(bugs, security, wiring, hygiene), reports in `docs/gpt/gauntlet/`. Fix every CRITICAL and
   106	HIGH, commit, re-run until all four say GO, log the folder + verdicts. One family only:
   107	it complements, never replaces, the harness's cross-family panel.
   108	
   109	**Shipping:** push your `gpt/*` branch (the pre-push hook — `./scripts/install-hooks.sh`
   110	once per clone — runs the tests and checks `./scripts/mark-reviewed.sh` was run on this
   111	exact commit). Merging to `main` is the harness's job, after its own cross-family review.
   112	
   113	**When stuck:** do less, not more — finish what is safe, log exactly what is unfinished
   114	and why, and stop.
   115	
   116	## The architecture in one paragraph
   117	
   118	A **fast head** (Talker) is what Neill talks to — non-reasoning, excellent tool-calling,
   119	sub-second. It never thinks; it dispatches. Heavy work goes to **reasoner seats** via
   120	OpenClaw's `sessions_spawn`, which is **non-blocking** — it returns a run id immediately,
   121	so the head never waits and can keep talking. Deep reasoning is a tool the head reaches
   122	for on demand, not a tax on every sentence. Long builds grind in the background on
   123	flat-rate subscription seats. Every dispatched job is recorded in SQLite so
   124	*"what did the overnight run do?"* is answerable.
